WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.510 align:start position:0%
 
this is the book that helped me escape  my childhood this is the book that i  read when no one in my family or friends  was as mind-blown as i was by how the  universe came to be or how physics works  or how quantum mechanics works this is  the book that started my fascination  with the human brain and made me really  wanted to study neurology for the first  time i've probably read thousands of  books by this point in my life and i  often get asked how to make reading a  habit or how to eat more and usually my  response is well why do you want to do  this at all and the answer to that is  something along the lines of you know  it's good for you while i don't think  that anyone should feel guilty that they  read more or less than other people i  genuinely think that books have changed  the way that my brain works and  therefore i want to share five ways that  books have kind of shifted and changed  my brain and if any one of these sounds  a bit interesting maybe that might give  you a stronger incentive to start  reading number one i think that books  give you the opportunity to have more  friends and as sad as this sounds let me  try to explain especially when i was  younger i did not really have access to  a lot of people it would just be my  family and people that i was with in  school and the chance is that in this  very small group of people you find  someone who matches with each one of  your preferences in life is actually  quite low so when it comes to books i  find that if someone has thought about  it someone else or them has probably  written about it so i don't like the  thought that we should limit our  experiences and interactions with people  with just those with whom we kind of run  into in our day-to-day life we should  kind of broaden that scope into people  who are somewhere else in the world or  have even died and are not alive today  and books definitely allow you to do  this especially non-fiction books feel a  lot like a conversation and therefore  you can kind of explore topics that you  are interested in with someone else with  whom you probably will never get the  chance to meet but that's absolutely  fine because you're getting the pleasure  of their kind of friendship and  interaction you actually might find that  you have a lot more in common with a  philosopher who died 300 years ago in  some ways that you view the world than  with your flatmate i have definitely  formed friendships in some ways with  certain authors or at least their essays  or things that they have written i love  the sentence that we only feel lonely  because experiences like ours are  scattered in the world and actually we  are not that alone and i feel like books  can kind of help unlock that when you  see even with fictional characters  things that people have gone through  might be very similar to things that you  have experienced but no one around you  has done the same thing i feel as though  books in the past have definitely helped  me feel a lot less alone secondly and i  love this one books have created a whole  other fictional reality in my brain i  will use harry potter as an example for  this or i could use aragon for the same  kind of thing but books go into so much  detail about whole other worlds so in my  brain i have this kind of space for  people and places and laws and  connections and histories and stories  that are just as real in a way as the  world i have lived in myself so  sometimes even as an adult if i just  want to kind of escape the present for  like half an hour or an hour all i need  to do is pick up a harry potter book and  i know that i can very easily dip into  this whole other reality which makes so  much sense for me and beyond this i  actually remember what was happening in  my life when i was first reading the  book and laying down these memories so  this like harry potter world for example  is kind of very intertwined with my own  personal life and my own personal story  and so it kind of adds layers and  dimension to my own personal memories of  my life experience and also adds the  whole areas of vibrancy and joy that are  very fun to kind of escape to when i  want to and i think it kind of really  promotes my imagination and prompts my  memories in a way that is absolutely  beautiful 10 out of 10 would recommend  reading fiction for this the third thing  is that books kind of switch on a radio  in my brain very often and what i mean  by this is for example you might be in a  empty quiet room and you might think  okay there is literally no sound in here  but if someone gave you radio and you  tuned it on to the right frequency you  can think oh yes there must be radio  waves because now i am hearing the sound  through the radio and reading books can  sometimes have this effect on me so for  example if i'm reading a story wherein  the author is kind of describing the  trees and a lot of detail and the way  that they move and the way that they  look and the way that their marks are  shaped and grow i feel as though the  next time i leave my house and look at  the tree i will appreciate it in a way  that i have never done before because  many different authors choose to focus  on different aspects of reality which  can be physical things such as trees or  certain ways that we experience the  world or certain characters or  characteristics about people like the  way that the hair flows in the main  protagonist or the way that um someone's  eyes sparkle i start to notice these  things in my own reality for a short  period of time after so i somehow adopt  the lens that the author uses to view  the world as my own and this can be  extremely fun it definitely adds like  vibrancy in my way of experiencing the  world and i can always tell after a book  i'm having the after effects of the  author's way of writing in my own  experience so i find that to be  extremely extremely fun the next thing  that books have done for me is kind of  unlock parts of my brain that i did not  have access to for example sometimes if  i'm feeling like bad i might name that  feeling as anxiety or stress or just  sadness but very often it feels quite  vague i don't actually know what is  causing it or what the feeling is i  fully believe that if i don't have  specific words to name feelings i just  don't have access to what that feeling  really is in the higher parts of my  brain so my subconscious might know what  it is and i but i am only consciously  aware that there's something brewing  down there but i don't know what it is  and sometimes i will read a book wherein  the author has an ability to describe  something very very similar to what i'm  feeling and suddenly something clicks in  my brain and i go oh my god yes that's  what i was feeling or that is why i did  that thing or that is why i felt a  certain way and i feel like authors have  such a great ability to do this probably  because they spend so much time like  years or months building up characters  and their experiences or because like i  said no one around me has gone through  this experience but the author has done  and so they've put it in a character in  their book or just because they have a  better grasp of literature and language  than i do so they just tend to name  things in a way that makes me unlock  that experience unlock that feeling in a  way that i couldn't do before if you're  looking for this sort of experience i  would definitely recommend classics  people like virginia woolf or  shakespeare have given i think so much  time into analyzing the human experience  and they are so gifted with words in  describing what they're thinking so well  that very very often with these two  authors i will find that kind of click  in my brain when i go oh my god yes this  is it of course you can get this effect  or much better effects through therapy  but i find to a lesser extent to get the  same thing through reading and my fifth  and final thing is that books have  helped me learn about my own brain and  what i mean by this is that i fully  believe that if two people were to read  the same book they would read it in two  completely different ways the reason for  that is every time i approach a book i  approach it with my own background with  my own judgment with my own baggage and  with my own ways of viewing things  therefore very often i will learn more  about myself from a book than i learned  from the book itself sometimes i will  sometimes i will read into something and  think oh this was definitely not what  the author meant but this is what i feel  and so the book will just kind of be the  key that unlocks that thing within  myself sometimes that will be blatantly  obvious sometimes it is a lot less  obvious and it will only come about when  i'm reading the summary of the book or  reading a forum on the book or if i'm  lucky enough to have someone else who  has read the book around me to discuss  it with them and realize maybe this is  just me and not what the author meant i  feel as though this path of  unintentional self-exploration that  books can put me on is absolutely  beautiful and i feel as though fiction  and philosophy are the best things for  this when it comes to myself i wanted to  keep this video short and sweet i'm  going to end it here but i hope any of  these made sense to you and if you could  please share your current struggles with  reading i would actually love to  potentially do a video on overcoming  those which might go in a bit more depth  but if you made it so far thank you so  much for spending this time with me i  hope you have a wonderful rest of your  day be kind yourself and others and  don't believe everything you think  thanks bye

